window.ORIG.push(
{ id: 'd20261107-1031-1', kind: 'Relato', topic: 'life', title: 'The Ledger in the Wardrobe', sub: 'A house clearer in east London finds nine notebooks in a dead man’s flat, and a debt of twenty pounds that has gone unpaid for thirty-six years. A story about kindness, pride and the things we never quite manage to say.',
body: `The flat was on the fourth floor of a block in Walthamstow that had been built in the sixties by people who clearly believed the future would be bright, uncomplicated and painted mustard yellow. The lift had been broken since August. Nell Achterberg carried the first stack of flattened boxes up the stairs with a roll of bin bags hooked over her wrist like a bracelet, and by the third landing she had stopped pretending she wasn't out of breath.

Dev was already inside, standing in the middle of the sitting room with his hands on his hips, the way he always stood at the start of a job. He said it helped him see the place as a set of problems rather than as somebody's life.

"Not bad," he said. "Tidy. Bit of brown furniture. Nothing a charity will want. Two days, maybe a day and a half if you don't stop to read everything."

"I don't read everything."

"You read everything, Nell. You read the backs of bus tickets."

He wasn't wrong. She had been clearing houses for Dev for three years, ever since she had dropped out of a history degree she could no longer afford, and she had never quite learnt to treat the things in a dead person's home as stock. Dev had been doing it for twenty-two years and treated everything as stock, including, she sometimes suspected, his own family. It made him quick. It made him good at the job. It made the job bearable, she supposed, which was more than she could say for her own method.

The flat had belonged to a man called Arthur Penrose, who had died in the spring at the age of eighty-one. The council had found him, eventually, after a neighbour reported that his post was piling up behind the door. There was a son, Dev said, somewhere in Surrey, who had instructed them by email and had made it plain that he wanted the place emptied and the keys handed back to the housing association as quickly and as cheaply as possible.

"Anything worth anything, we put aside for him. Everything else goes. He doesn't want photos, doesn't want papers, doesn't want to know." Dev shrugged. "His words, more or less. Some people are like that. Not our business."

Nell began in the kitchen, because kitchens were easy. A man who lived alone for a long time leaves a particular kind of kitchen: one good knife, sharpened to almost nothing; a single saucepan with a burnt bottom; a cupboard of tins arranged by the date he had bought them rather than the date they expired. Arthur Penrose had liked pilchards, custard creams and a brand of instant coffee she had not seen since her grandmother's house in Leiden. He had kept every rubber band that the postman had ever dropped on the mat, looped around the handle of a drawer, so many of them that they formed a sort of red, fleshy ball.

By eleven she had moved on to the bedroom, and that was where she found the ledgers.

There were nine of them, the old-fashioned kind with marbled covers and red cloth spines, stacked in the bottom of the wardrobe beneath a pair of shoes that had been polished so often the leather had gone soft as a glove. She lifted the first one out, expecting accounts: the gas bill, the rent, the sad arithmetic of a state pension. Instead, on the first page, in a neat, upright hand, she read:

3rd March 1971. Woman at the bus stop on Hoe Street lent me 2p for the phone when mine was swallowed. Would not give her address. Owed: 2p and a thank you.

She turned the page.

19th March 1971. Mr Kaplan at the ironmonger's knocked a shilling off the hinges because I was "a young man starting out". Owed: one shilling and a good word, said to someone else.

And then, further down:

8th April 1971. Joan made shepherd's pie. Burnt at the edges, which I like. She remembered. Owed: everything, really.

Nell sat down on the edge of the bed. The mattress sighed. She went on reading, and the morning went with it.

The ledgers ran from 1971 until the January before he died. Every entry followed the same pattern: a date, a small kindness that someone had done for Arthur Penrose, and what he believed he owed in return. Some of the debts had been struck through with a single line and a note beside them: Paid, 12th June. Took her a bunch of sweet peas. Or: Paid, in a manner of speaking. Did the same for a lad at Euston. Others were not struck through, and beside some of these, years later, he had added in a shakier hand: Too late. Moved away. Or simply: Died.

There were entries about strangers who had helped him with a flat tyre, colleagues who had covered for him when he was ill, a doctor who had stayed late. There were a great many entries about Joan, who seemed to be his wife, until 1994, after which there were none about her, and for several months none at all.

And there were entries about Martin.

14th July 1979. Martin, aged six, gave me his last fruit pastille because I "looked sad". I was sad. Owed: I don't know how you repay a thing like that.

2nd November 1985. Martin stayed up to wait for me after the late shift. Fell asleep on the stairs. Owed: more time. Must give him more time.

21st May 1990. Martin lent me twenty pounds from his paper round money when the car went. Said I wasn't to worry about it. Owed: £20. PAY HIM BACK.

That last one was not struck through. Beneath it, in a different pen, Arthur had written a date in 1996 and the words: Tried. He sent the cheque back. And beneath that, in the shaky hand of the last few years: Still owed.

"Nell." Dev was in the doorway. "It's half twelve. What have you got?"

"Nothing," she said, then, because that was a lie: "Notebooks."

"Bin."

"Dev, they're not just notebooks."

He came over and looked, briefly, the way he looked at everything, assessing it for weight and resale. He read a line or two. Something passed across his face and was gone before she could name it.

"The son said no papers," he said. "That's the instruction. We're not a museum." He put the ledger back in her hands rather than in the bin bag, though, and she noticed that.

She did not throw them away. She put them in a box of their own and wrote on the side, in marker pen, KEEP, and then, after a moment's thought, a question mark.

That evening, on the bus home, she found she could not stop thinking about the twenty pounds.

It was not a large sum. It was the sort of money people lose down the back of the sofa, or spend on a takeaway without noticing. But Arthur Penrose had carried it for thirty-six years, through his wife's death and his own retirement and whatever had happened between him and his son, and at the end of his life he had still written it down: Still owed. Nell had spent her childhood in a house where money was so scarce it had a presence of its own, like a lodger nobody liked, and she understood that a debt was never really about the amount. It was about what you could and could not say to the person you owed.

She also understood that it was none of her business. Dev had been clear. The son had been clear. The job was to empty the flat, not to rummage around in a dead man's conscience.

She emailed Martin Penrose at ten o'clock that night, from her own address rather than the company's, which she knew was a sackable offence and did anyway.

Dear Mr Penrose, she wrote. I'm one of the team clearing your father's flat. I know you asked us to dispose of all papers and I will if that's what you want. But among his things there are some notebooks I think you may want to see before you decide. I'm sorry to bother you. Nell.

She deleted the I'm sorry to bother you, then put it back, then deleted it again and sent it.

He replied at seven the next morning, which told her he had either not slept or was the kind of man who checked his phone before he got out of bed.

I don't want anything of his. Please follow the instructions you've been given. M. Penrose.

That, she thought, was that.

She was halfway through the sitting room when the doorbell went. It was the kind of bell that played a tune, or tried to, and gave up after four notes. Dev was downstairs loading the van. Nell wiped her hands on her jeans and opened the door to a man of about fifty in a navy overcoat, with the face of someone who had driven a long way while arguing with himself.

"You're Nell," he said. It was not a question.

"Mr Penrose."

"I was coming up anyway," he said, too quickly. "To drop off the second set of keys. I didn't need to. I could have posted them." He looked past her into the flat and his jaw tightened. "God, it still smells the same. Pilchards."

"He had eleven tins," she said, and to her surprise he almost laughed.

She made him a coffee with the last of Arthur's instant, in the only two mugs she had not yet packed, and they stood in the kitchen because all the chairs were already in the van. He did not ask about the notebooks. She did not mention them. They talked about the traffic on the M25 and the lift and the housing association, and it was like watching two people walk around the edge of a hole in the floor.

In the end it was Martin who broke.

"What's in them?" he said. "The notebooks. Is it about me? Because if it's some long account of how I let him down, I genuinely don't need to read it. I've heard it. I heard it for twenty years."

"It's not about how you let him down," Nell said carefully. "It's about the opposite, mostly."

She went to the bedroom and brought back the box. He looked at the word KEEP and the question mark beside it for a long moment. Then he took out the first ledger and opened it, and she watched his face change as he read.

He read the way people read letters from hospital: very slowly, then very fast, then going back to the beginning to check he had understood. He turned pages until he found Joan, and his mouth twisted. He kept going until he found himself.

"The fruit pastille," he said quietly. "I don't remember that."

"He did."

He found the twenty pounds. He read the entry once, and the note underneath it, and then the last line, Still owed, and he put the book down on the worktop very gently, as though it might go off.

"He tried to give me that money back," he said. "In 1996. Did you know that? He sent me a cheque for twenty pounds in a card. No letter. Just the cheque and his name. Two years after Mum died, two years after he'd barely said a word to me at the funeral, and he sends me twenty quid like a bloody, I don't know, a parking fine. I was so angry I couldn't see straight. I tore it up and posted it back to him."

Nell said nothing. She had found, over three years of empty flats, that the most useful thing you could do for a grieving person was usually nothing at all, done patiently.

"I thought he was trying to settle up," Martin said. "Close the account. Draw a line under me. That's what it felt like. As if the only thing between us that mattered was money."

He picked the ledger up again and looked at the shaky line at the bottom of the page.

"He wasn't trying to close it, was he? He was trying to open it."

"I think," Nell said, "he didn't know how to say the thing, so he tried to pay the thing instead. My dad was the same. He once fixed my bike chain instead of apologising for missing my graduation. From school, I mean. I was sixteen. It took me ten years to work out that the bike chain was the apology."

Martin wiped his face with the heel of his hand, quickly, as if he could do it without either of them noticing.

"Why are you doing this?" he asked. "You don't know me. You didn't know him. You could have just binned the lot. That's what I asked you to do."

It was a fair question, and she answered it honestly.

"Because I nearly did," she said. "And then I thought about all the people in those books he never managed to pay back. The woman at the bus stop. Mr Kaplan at the ironmonger's. He spent fifty years trying to square things with people, and most of them he couldn't find. You're the only one left who's still here."

Dev came up the stairs at that moment, breathing like a train, and stopped in the doorway when he saw them. He took in the box, the open ledger, the son, and Nell, and the fact that she had plainly ignored every instruction she had been given. She braced herself.

"Mr Penrose," Dev said instead, holding out a hand. "Sorry for your loss." Then, to Nell, without looking at her: "Van's full. I'll do a run to the tip. Take your time."

When the van had gone, Martin sat down on the floor of the empty sitting room, his back against the wall where a picture had once hung and left a paler rectangle behind it, and read for nearly two hours. Nell worked around him. She cleared the bathroom cabinet and took down the curtains, and occasionally he read something aloud, not to her exactly but to the room.

"Listen to this. 'Martin got the scholarship. I said well done in the wrong voice. He thinks I'm not proud. Owed: to tell him. Do it at Christmas.'" He let out a long breath. "He didn't do it at Christmas."

"No."

"He didn't do it ever."

"He wrote it down, though," Nell said. "Every time. He knew."

"Knowing isn't the same as saying."

"No," she agreed. "It isn't."

At about four o'clock, as the light was going, Martin stood up and stretched, and his knees cracked like his father's must have done on those stairs. He put the ledgers back in the box, carefully, in order.

"I'll take them," he said. "All of them." He picked up the box and then put it down again. "Can I ask you something odd?"

"Everyone does, in this job."

"The woman at the bus stop. The two pence. The ironmonger. Do you think there's any way to find any of them? Or their families?"

Nell thought about it. "Some of them, maybe. Mr Kaplan's shop is probably a phone shop now. But there'll be records. Electoral rolls. Local history groups. People are easier to find than you'd think, if you're patient."

"I've got time," Martin said. "Turns out I've got a lot of time. My kids are at university and they don't ring me either." He gave a short, rueful laugh. "Runs in the family, apparently."

"It doesn't have to," she said.

He looked at her as if she had said something much more important than she had meant to.

Before he left, he took out his wallet. Nell assumed, with a small, tired sinking feeling, that he was going to tip her, and she was already preparing to refuse it when he took out a twenty-pound note, folded it once, and tucked it inside the front cover of the 1990 ledger, on the page with the entry about the paper round and the car.

"There," he said. "Paid."

Then he took a pen from his coat, the expensive kind, and beneath his father's last line, Still owed, he wrote in his own hand: Forgiven. And then, after a moment, he crossed that out and wrote instead: Nothing was owed, Dad. You were my father.

He showed it to her, almost shyly, and she nodded, because there was nothing at all she could have added to it.

Dev came back from the tip at half five and found her sweeping the empty flat. He looked around at the bare floors, the pale rectangles on the walls, the hooks with nothing on them.

"The son took the notebooks, then."

"He did."

"Against his own instructions."

"People change their instructions," Nell said, "if you give them a reason."

Dev grunted. He went to the window and looked out at the car park, where the streetlights were coming on one by one, and for a while he said nothing. Then, without turning round, he said: "My old man kept a list like that. Not kindnesses. Grudges. Every person who'd ever crossed him, in a book in the kitchen drawer. When he died I burnt it without reading it." He paused. "I've always wondered if my name was in it."

"Would it have mattered?"

"I don't know," Dev said. "That's rather the point, isn't it. I'll never know."

They locked up together and carried the last bin bags down the four flights of stairs, and in the van on the way back Dev turned the radio off, which he never did, and asked her, out of nowhere, what she had been studying before she left university.

"History," she said.

"Right," he said, as if that explained a great deal. "Right. Makes sense."

Nell kept a notebook after that. Not a ledger exactly, and not every day, but whenever someone did something kind for her that she did not know how to repay, she wrote it down, with the date. A man on the Victoria line who gave her his seat when she had flu. Her landlady, who let the rent slide for a month in February. Dev, who put her name on the company insurance and then, the following spring, without being asked, on a pay rise.

Beside each one she wrote what she thought she owed. And she tried, as Arthur Penrose had tried, to settle each debt while the person was still there to receive it. She did not always manage. But she found that the trying changed her more than the paying ever could, and that most of the time, when she finally said the thing she had been meaning to say, the other person looked at her in surprise and told her she owed them nothing at all.

She never quite believed them. She was not sure she was supposed to.` },
{ id: 'd20261107-1031-2', kind: 'Relato', topic: 'myth', title: 'Gilgamesh and the Plant of Youth', sub: 'The oldest great story in the world, retold at length: a tyrant king, a wild man made of clay, a friendship, a death, and a journey to the edge of the world in search of a cure for mortality.',
body: `In the land between the two rivers, where the Tigris and the Euphrates run down to the sea through mud and reeds and blinding light, there was once a city called Uruk, and in Uruk there was a king who could not sleep.

His name was Gilgamesh. The poets who sang about him said he was two-thirds god and one-third man. His mother was the wise goddess Ninsun, his father a king of old, and the gods had given him everything a body could hold: beauty, strength, a voice that carried across the marketplace, a mind that missed nothing. He had built the walls of Uruk himself, or so he liked to say, great walls of baked brick that ran for miles around the city, gleaming like copper in the evening sun. Nobody in the world was his equal, and that was precisely the problem.

Because he had no equal, he had no limit. He ran through the city by day and by night with his young men, wrestling, racing, drumming, and he let no son go home to his father. He took the brides of Uruk on their wedding nights before their husbands could. He was magnificent and he was exhausting, and the people of Uruk began to pray in whispers that someone, anyone, would stop him.

The gods heard, and for once they listened. They went to Aruru, the mother goddess who had shaped humankind in the beginning, and they said to her: "You made him. Now make his match. Make a second Gilgamesh, a storm to meet his storm, so that the two of them can wear each other out and leave the city in peace."

Aruru washed her hands, pinched off a piece of clay, and threw it down into the wilderness. Where it landed, a man rose up.

The wild man

He was called Enkidu, and he knew nothing of cities. His body was covered in hair, and his hair hung down his back like a woman's, thick as barley. He ate grass with the gazelles and drank at the waterholes with the wild beasts, jostling among them as one of them. He did not know what bread was, or beer, or a word.

One day a trapper went to check his pits and found them filled in and his snares torn up, and standing by the waterhole, head and shoulders above the herd, was a man more terrifying than anything he had ever seen. He ran home to his father, white to the lips, and the old man, who had seen a great deal of the world, thought about it. "Go to Uruk," he said. "Tell Gilgamesh. Ask him for Shamhat, the woman of the temple, and take her to the waterhole. When the wild man sees her, he will go to her. And when he has been with her, the animals will no longer know him."

So the trapper went to Uruk, and Gilgamesh, amused, gave him what he asked. On the third day of waiting the herd came down to drink, and Enkidu with them. Shamhat did not run. She showed herself to him, and Enkidu, who had never seen a woman, forgot the gazelles entirely. For six days and seven nights he stayed with her. And when at last he rose and went back to the herd, the gazelles saw him coming and scattered, and the wild beasts of the plain ran from him. His legs had grown slow, his body heavy. Something had been lost. But something had been gained as well, because when he walked back to Shamhat and sat at her feet, he found he could understand her when she spoke.

"You are wise now, Enkidu," she said. "You are like a god. Why do you run with the animals? Come with me to Uruk, the city of the sheepfold, where Gilgamesh is king and lords it over the people like a wild bull."

And Enkidu, who for the first time in his life felt lonely, said: "Take me there. I will challenge him. I will shout in Uruk: I am the strongest. I have come to change the order of things."

On the way, Shamhat took him to a camp of shepherds. They put bread in front of him and he stared at it, not knowing what to do. They gave him beer, and he drank seven jugs of it, and his heart sang and his face shone. He washed. He oiled his body. He put on clothes and became, the poem says, a man.

The fight in the doorway

It was a wedding night when Enkidu reached Uruk, and Gilgamesh was on his way to the house of the bride. The people had gathered in the street, whispering, and when they saw the newcomer they said to one another: "He is the image of Gilgamesh. He is shorter, but his bones are stronger. He was raised on the milk of wild animals."

Enkidu planted himself in the doorway of the wedding house and barred the king's way.

Nobody had ever done that before. Gilgamesh stopped. For a moment the two men looked at each other, the one born to be a god and the one made from a handful of mud, and then they seized each other like bulls. The doorposts shook and the walls trembled while the whole of Uruk watched, until at last Gilgamesh, with one knee on the ground, held his opponent, and his anger left him all at once, the way a fever breaks.

Enkidu spoke first. "Your mother bore you to be unique," he said. "Enlil himself gave you kingship over the people. There is no one like you."

And Gilgamesh, who had never in his life been spoken to as an equal, embraced him. They kissed each other. They became friends.

The gods had sent Enkidu to defeat Gilgamesh, and in a sense he did, not by beating him but by giving him something he had never had: somebody to love who was not afraid of him. The king's terrible energy had found somewhere else to go.

The forest of cedars

Unfortunately, it went straight into the next adventure.

"Far away to the west," Gilgamesh said one day, "there is a forest of cedars, and in it lives a monster called Humbaba, whom Enlil appointed to guard it. His roar is the flood, his mouth is fire, his breath is death. Let us go and kill him, you and I, and cut down the cedars, and make a name for ourselves that will last for ever."

Enkidu went pale. "It is not a fight anyone can win," he said.

But Gilgamesh had made up his mind, and when he had made up his mind there was very little anyone could do. "Who can climb to heaven, my friend?" he said. "Only the gods live for ever. As for men, their days are numbered, and whatever they do is wind. You are afraid of death already, and you have not even started. Let me go in front. If I fall, at least I will have made a name. People will say: Gilgamesh died fighting the monster."

That speech is the key to everything that follows. Gilgamesh already knew he would die. What he could not bear was to die and be forgotten.

So the smiths of Uruk cast them great axes, and his mother Ninsun prayed to the sun god Shamash to protect her reckless son. Every night on the road Gilgamesh had terrible dreams of falling mountains and fire, and every morning Enkidu, with the loyalty of a friend who will say anything to keep you going, told him they were good omens.

At last they came to the edge of the forest. The cedars rose up before them like a wall of green, their scent so rich it seemed to fill the air with oil, and somewhere deep inside, something enormous was moving.

Humbaba came out to meet them. He was everything the stories had said, and the two heroes, for all their talk, were terrified. But the sun god Shamash was on their side, and he sent thirteen winds against the monster, the south wind and the north wind, the whirlwind and the icy wind and the scorching wind, and they pinned Humbaba where he stood, so that he could neither run forwards nor back.

And then Humbaba begged for his life.

"Gilgamesh," he said, "let me live. I will be your servant. I will cut down as many cedars as you want for you. I will guard them for you. Only spare me."

Gilgamesh hesitated. For a moment he might have spared him. It was Enkidu, the wild man, the friend of animals, who said: "Do not listen to him, my friend. Kill him before the gods find out. Kill him now and make your name."

Humbaba turned his terrible eyes on Enkidu and cursed him. "May you not grow old," he said. "May Enkidu never find peace in this world." Then Gilgamesh struck him in the neck, and Enkidu struck too, and the guardian of the forest fell.

They cut down the cedars, the tallest whose top touched the sky, and from its trunk Enkidu made a great door to send down the river to the temple of Enlil in Nippur, as if a gift could make up for what they had done. Then they built a raft and floated home in triumph, Gilgamesh holding the monster's head.

The goddess and the bull

Back in Uruk, Gilgamesh washed the dust of the journey from his hair, and when he put on his crown he looked so splendid that Ishtar, the goddess of love and war, looked down from heaven and wanted him.

"Come, Gilgamesh," she said. "Be my husband. Give me your body as a gift. I will harness a chariot of lapis lazuli and gold for you. Kings will bow down before you."

Any sensible man would have said yes, or at least said no politely. Gilgamesh laughed at her. He called her a draughty door, a shoe that pinched its wearer, and then, one by one, he listed her former lovers and what had become of them: Tammuz, for whom she decreed endless weeping; the little bird whose wing she broke; the horse she sentenced to the whip; the shepherd she turned into a wolf, so that his own dogs bit his legs.

"And if you loved me," Gilgamesh said, "you would treat me exactly the same."

Ishtar went up to heaven in a fury and wept in front of her father Anu. "Give me the Bull of Heaven," she said, "so that I can kill Gilgamesh in his own house. If you do not, I will smash the gates of the underworld and raise up the dead to eat the living."

Anu gave her the bull.

It came down on Uruk like a disaster. When it snorted, a pit opened in the ground and a hundred young men fell into it. The third time, Enkidu fell in up to his waist, but he leapt out and seized the bull by the horns and shouted to his friend: "Now! Thrust your sword between the neck and the horns!" And Gilgamesh, like a butcher, drove his sword in.

They tore out the bull's heart and offered it to Shamash. Ishtar, standing on the walls of Uruk, howled with grief and rage. And Enkidu, drunk on victory, ripped off one of the bull's thighs and flung it at her face. "If I could catch you," he shouted, "I would do the same to you. I would hang its guts on your arms."

It was the last happy night of his life.

The death of Enkidu

Enkidu dreamt that the gods were sitting in council. "They have killed Humbaba," Anu said, "and they have killed the Bull of Heaven. One of them must die." And Enlil said: "Let it be Enkidu. Gilgamesh shall not die."

In the morning Enkidu told his friend the dream, and then he fell ill.

He lay in bed for twelve days, growing weaker, and in his fever he raged against everything that had brought him there. He cursed the cedar door and the trapper who had found him. Most bitterly of all he cursed Shamhat, the woman who had taken him from the wild and taught him to be a man, because if she had left him alone with the gazelles he would never have come to this.

Then the sun god Shamash spoke to him from the sky. "Why do you curse Shamhat, Enkidu? She gave you bread fit for a god and beer fit for a king. She dressed you in splendid clothes. She gave you Gilgamesh as your companion. And now he will lay you on a great bed, and the people of Uruk will weep for you, and after you are gone he will let his hair grow long and wander the wilderness in a lion's skin."

Enkidu was quiet for a long time. Then he took back his curse, and he blessed Shamhat instead: may kings and princes love her, may men untie their purses for her, may she never lack for anything.

It is a small moment, but it may be the wisest in the whole poem. A dying man, furious at the life that is killing him, is reminded of what that life gave him, and chooses gratitude.

Before the end, Enkidu dreamt of the underworld. He saw himself seized and dragged down to the house of dust, where those who enter never leave, where the dead sit in darkness wearing feathers like birds, where dust is their food and clay their bread. Kings who had ruled the earth sat there as servants. There was no light. There was no return.

On the twelfth day, he died.

Gilgamesh would not believe it. He sat beside the body and talked to it. He touched his friend's heart and felt nothing. Then he began to circle the bed like a lioness whose cubs have been taken, tearing out his hair and ripping off his fine clothes. For six days and seven nights he refused to let anyone bury Enkidu, until, as the poem tells us with brutal honesty, a maggot dropped from his friend's nose. Only then did he let him go.

He had a statue made of Enkidu in gold and lapis lazuli. And then, exactly as Shamash had foretold, he let his hair grow wild, dressed himself in the skin of a lion, and walked out of Uruk into the desert.

The man at the edge of the world

He was not only grieving. He was terrified. "Must I die too?" he kept asking himself. "Must I become like Enkidu? Fear has entered my belly."

There was, he knew, one man in all the world who had escaped death. His name was Utnapishtim, and long ago, before the great flood, the gods had granted him eternal life and sent him to live far away, at the mouth of the rivers, beyond the edge of the world. If anyone knew the secret, he did.

So Gilgamesh went to find him.

He crossed mountains and deserts and killed lions in the passes at night. At the twin peaks of Mount Mashu, where the sun rises and sets, scorpion-people whose glance was death told him that no man had ever gone through the tunnel beneath. He went through it anyway: twelve double-hours of absolute darkness, until at last the light broke and he stepped out into a garden where the trees bore jewels for fruit.

He did not stop to look. On the shore of the sea he found a tavern, and in it a woman called Siduri who kept the ale-house there and served the gods. When she saw a wild, sun-blackened stranger in a lion's skin coming towards her, she locked the door.

Gilgamesh threatened to break it down, and she spoke to him through it. Then, when she had heard his story, she let him in. In one of the oldest versions of the poem, written down in the Old Babylonian period, nearly four thousand years ago, she gives him the advice that people have been quoting ever since.

"Gilgamesh, where are you wandering?" she says. "The life you seek you will not find. When the gods created mankind, they kept eternal life for themselves and gave death to men. So fill your belly. Make merry day and night. Dance and play. Let your clothes be clean, wash your head, bathe in water. Look at the little one who holds your hand, and let your wife delight in your embrace. This is the task of mankind."

Gilgamesh did not listen. He wanted the ferryman.

The ferryman was called Urshanabi, and he alone could cross the waters of death, which no living man could touch. Gilgamesh, in his impatience, smashed the mysterious "stone ones" that helped him make the crossing, and so he had to cut long punting poles and use each only once, so that not a drop of the deadly water touched his hand. When the poles ran out, he held up his lion skin as a sail.

The story of the flood

Gilgamesh had expected a god. What he found was an old man who looked like anyone else.

"I look at you, Utnapishtim," he said, "and you are no different from me. I had made up my mind to fight you, and instead I find myself lying idle on my back. Tell me how you came to stand among the gods and find eternal life."

And Utnapishtim told him.

Long ago, he said, in the city of Shuruppak, the gods decided to send a flood to destroy humanity. They swore an oath to keep it secret. But Ea, the clever god of fresh water, who loved mankind, came to the reed hut where Utnapishtim slept and, without breaking his oath, whispered to the wall: "Reed wall, reed wall. Wall, wall. Listen, reed wall. Man of Shuruppak, tear down your house and build a boat. Abandon wealth and seek life. Take aboard the seed of every living thing."

So Utnapishtim built a boat, a great cube of a boat with seven decks, sealed with pitch inside and out. He loaded his family aboard, and the craftsmen, and the beasts of the field, and when the evening came, the sky went black.

The storm lasted six days and seven nights. The dams of heaven burst and the land was smashed like a pot. Even the gods were afraid, and they fled up to the highest heaven and cowered there like dogs against a wall, and Ishtar screamed like a woman in childbirth, lamenting what she had helped to do. "The people I gave birth to," she cried, "now fill the sea like fish."

On the seventh day the storm stopped. Utnapishtim opened a window and the light fell on his face, and he looked out and saw that the sea was flat and silent and all of humankind had turned to clay. He sat down and wept.

The boat ran aground on a mountain called Nimush. After seven days he released a dove, and it flew away and came back, because it had found nowhere to land. He released a swallow, and it came back too. Then he released a raven, and the raven saw that the waters had gone down, and it ate and circled and did not return.

So Utnapishtim made an offering on the mountaintop, and the gods smelt the sweet savour and gathered round the sacrifice like flies. Enlil, who had sent the flood, was furious that anyone had survived. But Ea rebuked him for punishing the whole of humankind instead of only the guilty, and in the end Enlil touched the foreheads of Utnapishtim and his wife and declared that they should be like gods, and live far away, at the mouth of the rivers.

"That is how I came to live for ever," the old man said. "But who will call the gods together for you? Who will grant you the life you seek?"

Sleep, the little brother of death

Then he offered Gilgamesh a test. "If you want to conquer death," he said, "first conquer sleep. Stay awake for six days and seven nights."

Gilgamesh sat down on his heels to begin, and almost at once sleep blew over him like a mist.

Utnapishtim's wife felt sorry for him. "Wake him," she said, "let him go home." But Utnapishtim knew that men are liars, and that Gilgamesh would swear he had only closed his eyes for a moment. So he told his wife to bake a loaf of bread every day and set it by the sleeping man's head, and to mark the wall each day he slept.

When at last Utnapishtim touched him and he woke, Gilgamesh said, exactly as predicted: "I had hardly fallen asleep when you touched me."

"Count your loaves," said Utnapishtim.

The first was dry as a stone. The second was like leather. The third was soggy, the fourth white with mould, the fifth grey, the sixth still fresh. The seventh was just coming out of the oven.

Gilgamesh looked at the loaves for a long time. "What shall I do, Utnapishtim? Where shall I go? Death has taken hold of my bedroom. Wherever I set my feet, there is death."

The plant and the snake

Utnapishtim sent him home, washed and in clean clothes. But as they were pushing the boat off, Utnapishtim's wife spoke again. "He has come so far and suffered so much. What will you give him to take back to his land?"

So Utnapishtim told him one last secret. At the bottom of the sea, he said, there grows a plant with thorns like a rose, and whoever eats it will grow young again.

Gilgamesh did not hesitate. He dug a channel, tied heavy stones to his feet, and sank to the bottom of the sea. He seized the plant, though its thorns tore his hands. Then he cut the stones loose and the sea threw him up onto the shore.

"Urshanabi," he said, "this is a plant of great wonder. I will take it to Uruk and test it on an old man. I will call it 'The Old Man Becomes a Young Man'. Then I will eat it myself, and I will be as I was in my youth."

They travelled for many days. One evening they came to a pool of cool water, and Gilgamesh went down to bathe. While he was in the water, a snake smelt the fragrance of the plant. It rose up silently, carried it away, and as it went it shed its skin.

Gilgamesh sat down and wept. The tears ran down his face. "For whom have my hands laboured, Urshanabi? For whom have I spilt my heart's blood? I have not gained anything for myself. I have done a good deed for the snake."

So they went home. And as they came within sight of Uruk, Gilgamesh, the king who could not sleep, the man who had crossed the waters of death, turned to the ferryman and said something extraordinary. He did not speak of death at all. He said:

"Go up, Urshanabi, onto the walls of Uruk and walk around. Look at the foundations, inspect the brickwork. Is it not baked brick? Did not the seven sages lay its foundations? One square mile is city, one square mile is orchards, one square mile is clay pits, and half a square mile is the temple of Ishtar. This is Uruk."

The poem ends where it began, on the walls.

What the story means

The Epic of Gilgamesh is the oldest great work of literature that we possess. Stories about a king of Uruk called Gilgamesh, who may have been a real ruler around 2700 BC, were told in Sumerian more than four thousand years ago. The best-known version, on twelve clay tablets, was copied out for the library of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal at Nineveh, which was destroyed in 612 BC. The tablets lay in the ruins for more than two thousand years, until they were dug up and taken to the British Museum, where in 1872 a self-taught assistant called George Smith realised that one broken tablet told of a flood, a boat and birds sent out to look for dry land. Victorian Britain was electrified: a story strikingly like Noah's had been told in ancient Mesopotamia.

But the flood is not really the heart of the poem. The heart of it is a simple, devastating idea: a man discovers that he is going to die, and he cannot accept it.

Gilgamesh begins as a tyrant whose only problem is that he has no limits. He finds a friend and learns to love. He seeks glory because if your name lasts, perhaps you do too. Then his friend dies, and he learns that fame is no comfort at all when you are looking at the body of someone you love. So he looks for the real thing, eternal life, and he fails at every step. He cannot stay awake for a week, let alone for ever, and he loses the plant of youth to a snake while he is having a bath. The greatest hero in the world is defeated, in the end, by sleep and by a moment of carelessness, which is to say, by being human.

And yet the poem is not hopeless. Its two great pieces of advice are both quietly radical. Siduri tells him to enjoy what he has: food, clean clothes, the child holding his hand, the person he loves. And Gilgamesh, when he finally comes home, points not to his own body but to his walls, to the city he built, the orchards and the brickwork and the temple. He will die. Uruk will remain. What lasts is not the self but what the self makes, and the people it makes it for.

Every age has found its own reflection in this story: the first great tale of friendship, the first account of grief, the first quarrel between nature and civilisation, embodied in Enkidu, who gained a mind and lost the gazelles. In our own time, when the very rich spend fortunes on the dream of living for ever, it reads almost as satire.

Perhaps that is why it still matters. Four thousand years ago, someone in the land between the rivers looked death in the face and wrote down, on wet clay, the same questions we still ask in hospital corridors and at three in the morning. What is the point of all this, if it ends? And the answer they reached was not a trick or a magic plant. It was a wall, a meal, a hand to hold, and a friend worth weeping for.` },
{ id: 'd20261107-1031-3', kind: 'Noticia explicada', topic: 'politics', title: 'America’s midterm earthquake: what the Democrats’ victory means', sub: 'Democrats have taken both the House and the Senate halfway through Donald Trump’s second term. What happened on 3 November, why voters turned, and what a divided Washington will look like.',
body: `On Tuesday 3 November, Americans went to the polls in the midterm elections, the contests held halfway through a presidential term, and by the following morning the political map of Washington had been redrawn. The Democratic Party won control of the House of Representatives, which it had lost in 2022, and, more surprisingly, of the Senate, where Republicans had held a 53-47 majority. For the last two years of Donald Trump's second term, the president will face a Congress run by his opponents.

What happened

Every seat in the House and roughly a third of the Senate were on the ballot, along with dozens of governorships. Historically, the party that holds the White House tends to lose ground in midterms, so few analysts expected the Republicans to have a comfortable night. What surprised almost everyone was the scale of the defeat.

In the House, where a majority requires 218 seats, Democrats had flipped more than 40 Republican-held districts by midweek, according to CBS News, which projected that they were on course for a majority of around 255 seats. Several races were still being counted several days after polling day, so the final figure may shift, but the outcome is not in doubt.

The Senate was always the harder target. Democrats needed a net gain of four seats, and many of the seats in play were in states that Mr Trump had won comfortably. In the event, they won Republican-held seats across a remarkably wide stretch of the country. According to the Associated Press and other outlets, the former governor Roy Cooper won an open seat in North Carolina; James Talarico, a state legislator, defeated the Republican Ken Paxton in Texas; Sherrod Brown, a former senator, returned to Washington by beating Senator Jon Husted in Ohio; Adam Hamilton ousted Senator Roger Marshall in Kansas; Josh Turek beat Ashley Hinson in Iowa; and Annie Andrews won in South Carolina. In Maine, Troy Jackson defeated Senator Susan Collins, one of the chamber's best-known moderates, who conceded. Democrats also held on to the seats they were defending in Georgia, Michigan and New Hampshire.

CBS News projects that Democrats will have at least 54 seats when the new Senate meets in January. Two races remained undecided at the end of the week. In Alaska, which uses ranked-choice voting and is always slow to count, Senator Dan Sullivan faces the former congresswoman Mary Peltola. In Mississippi, CBS reported that Senator Cindy Hyde-Smith and her Democratic challenger, Scott Colom, were separated by fewer than 2,000 votes, with thousands of absentee and provisional ballots still to be processed. The state has until 13 November to certify the result, and both parties have sent lawyers.

There were striking results in the races for governor, too. Democrats won in Texas, where Gina Hinojosa defeated Governor Greg Abbott, and in Georgia, Ohio and Iowa. Republicans held Florida, where Byron Donalds was elected.

Why it happened

Exit polls and campaign strategists point to a familiar combination: prices and the president.

The cost of living dominated the campaign. In CBS News exit polling, 89 per cent of voters said it had been a major factor in their decision. Petrol prices, which had risen sharply during the war with Iran, were a constant theme, and The Hill noted that the tariffs introduced by the administration had also pushed up prices for ordinary goods. The Associated Press described voters' deep frustration with high prices, the war in Iran and concerns about government corruption.

The second factor was Mr Trump himself. The president was not on the ballot, but he dominated it. CBS's exit polls put his approval rating at 38 per cent, a figure the network described as historically low for a president at a midterm election. Democrats built much of their campaign around what they called affordability and accountability, and around the claim that the administration had become a "culture of corruption", a charge the White House rejects.

There is also a structural story. In the past two years, Republican-run states including Texas and Florida redrew their congressional maps in the middle of the decade, aiming to create more safe Republican seats. CBS News concluded that the strategy largely backfired: by spreading Republican voters more thinly across districts in order to win more of them, the new maps left many seats vulnerable in a year when the national mood swung against the party.

How each side sees it

Democrats are presenting the result as a clear mandate. Hakeem Jeffries, the party's leader in the House, who is expected to become Speaker in January, called it "a change election". Chuck Schumer, the Democratic leader in the Senate, called it a "generational remake of the Senate map" and promised that "whenever Trump defies the will of the American people or the law, we will launch investigations, take him to court, and entangle him in legislative razor wire."

Republicans, unsurprisingly, have a different reading. Mr Trump argued that the losses reflected his absence from the ballot rather than a verdict on his record. "If I was on the ballot, we would have won," he told reporters the day after the vote. Others in the party were more willing to name a cause. Vice-President JD Vance wrote on social media that "the reason we lost is the war", adding that the president himself had said so. Some Republicans in Congress were blunter still: Senator Ted Cruz, speaking on his podcast, called the result "not simply a blue wave" but "a blue tsunami".

The defeat has also set off a contest for the future of the House Republican conference. Mike Johnson, the outgoing Speaker, has said he will not run to lead the party in opposition. Steve Scalise, the current majority leader, and Jim Jordan, the chairman of the Judiciary Committee, are both seeking the post.

What happens next

The new Congress, the 120th, will be sworn in at the start of January. Until then, the current Republican majorities remain in place for what Americans call the "lame-duck" session, in which outgoing members can still pass legislation.

From January, the balance of power changes in three important ways.

First, legislation. Democrats have said their priorities include reversing cuts to Medicaid, the health programme for people on low incomes, and to food assistance that were passed by the Republican Congress, restoring subsidies for health insurance under the Affordable Care Act, rolling back tariffs and ending the war with Iran. They will be able to pass bills through both chambers, but Mr Trump can veto them, and overriding a veto requires a two-thirds majority in each chamber, which Democrats will not have. The likeliest result, on many issues, is deadlock, punctuated by hard bargaining over spending, since the government cannot be funded without the agreement of both Congress and the president.

Second, oversight. Control of the House gives Democrats control of its committees and their power to hold hearings and issue subpoenas. Robert Garcia, the Democrat expected to chair the House Oversight Committee, has said he intends to investigate the Trump family's business dealings, the conduct of the Department of Homeland Security and Immigration and Customs Enforcement, and the handling of the files relating to Jeffrey Epstein. A White House spokeswoman dismissed the plans as "sham investigations". Mr Jeffries has said that nobody in his caucus has raised the question of impeachment.

Third, appointments. The Senate confirms federal judges, cabinet members and many senior officials. With Democrats in charge of that process, the president will find it much harder to fill vacancies with nominees his opponents consider unacceptable, a change that could matter a great deal if a seat on the Supreme Court were to fall vacant.

Why it matters

For the rest of the world, the result matters because American foreign and trade policy may now face real constraints at home. A Congress that wants to end the war with Iran and rein in tariffs cannot simply dictate to the president, who retains wide powers in both areas, but it can make his life more difficult through funding, hearings and public pressure. Allies in Europe and trading partners in Asia will be watching to see how much the White House adjusts.

The election also sets the stage for 2028, when Mr Trump, barred by the Constitution from seeking a third term, will leave office. Democrats will go into that contest with momentum and a crop of new senators and governors from states they had not won in years. Republicans will go into it arguing over what went wrong and who should lead them. Midterm results are not always a reliable guide to the next presidential election: Barack Obama's party was routed in 2010, and he was comfortably re-elected two years later. But this one, by any measure, was a political earthquake, and its aftershocks will be felt for the next two years.

Sources: CBS News, the Associated Press (via MPR News), The Hill, Northeastern Global News.` },
{ id: 'd20261107-1031-4', kind: 'Ensayo', topic: 'ideas', title: 'Is an unjust law still law? Hart, Fuller and the grudge informer', sub: 'A woman denounced her husband under a Nazi statute. Was she obeying the law? The famous debate between an Oxford positivist and a Harvard natural lawyer, and why it still matters to every lawyer.',
body: `In 1944, in a small German town, a woman wanted to be rid of her husband. He was a soldier, home on leave, and in private conversation with her he had said some disparaging things about Hitler and the conduct of the war. She reported him. Under the laws then in force, making such remarks was a crime, and so was failing to report them. The husband was arrested, tried by a military court and sentenced to death. In the event the sentence was not carried out, and he was sent to the Eastern Front instead. He survived the war.

In 1949 it was the wife's turn to stand in the dock. She was prosecuted under the old German Criminal Code of 1871 for unlawfully depriving her husband of his liberty. Her defence was simple, and in its own terms difficult to answer. She had done nothing illegal. She had informed on him under a statute that was valid at the time, and the court that sentenced him had applied that statute correctly. How could she be guilty of a crime for obeying the law?

This was the so-called case of the grudge informer, and for a few years in the late 1950s it became the most argued-over case in the English-speaking world, even though it had not been decided in an English-speaking court and the version most people discussed turned out to be wrong. It mattered because it forced into the open one of the oldest questions in legal philosophy, a question that every law student meets in the first year and that most lawyers spend the rest of their careers trying not to think about. What is law? And, in particular, is an unjust law still law at all?

Two answers

There are, broadly speaking, two traditions, and each has a long pedigree.

The first is called natural law. It holds that law and morality are, at some deep level, connected: that there are standards of justice which do not depend on what any ruler or parliament decides, and that a command which flagrantly violates those standards is not really law, whatever it calls itself. The idea is as old as Western literature. In Sophocles' play, Antigone defies King Creon's decree that her brother's body must be left unburied, and she justifies herself by appealing to the unwritten and unfailing laws of the gods, which are not of today or yesterday but live for ever. Cicero argued that true law is right reason in agreement with nature. Saint Augustine suggested that a law that is not just does not seem to be a law at all, and Thomas Aquinas built on that idea in the thirteenth century: human laws that conflict with the natural law are, in his phrase, not so much laws as corruptions of law. The slogan that summarises the tradition, lex iniusta non est lex, an unjust law is no law, is usually traced to them. Martin Luther King quoted Augustine to exactly this effect in his Letter from Birmingham Jail in 1963, when he explained why he was prepared to break the segregation laws of Alabama.

The second tradition is called legal positivism, and it is younger, sharper and, in the English-speaking world at least, much more influential among practising lawyers. The word "positive" here has nothing to do with optimism; it comes from the Latin for "laid down". Positivists say that law is a social fact. Whether a rule is legally valid depends on whether it was made in the right way by the right people, not on whether it is good. Jeremy Bentham and his disciple John Austin, writing in the early nineteenth century, were the founders of the modern version, and Austin put the central claim in a sentence that has been quoted ever since: "The existence of law is one thing; its merit or demerit is another."

It is worth pausing on why the positivists thought this so important, because it is easy to caricature them as cold technicians who did not care about justice. Bentham cared about justice enormously. He was a furious reformer, and that was precisely why he wanted law and morality kept apart. If you believe that whatever the law says is, by definition, just, you lose the ability to criticise it. If you believe that whatever is just is, by definition, the law, you give every judge a licence to rewrite the statutes according to his own conscience. Only by keeping the two questions separate, what the law is and what it ought to be, can you see clearly enough to change it.

The two men

In the spring of 1957 an English philosopher called Herbert Hart crossed the Atlantic to deliver a lecture at Harvard Law School. Hart was then about fifty. He had practised at the Chancery Bar before the war, worked for British intelligence during it, and in 1952 had been elected to the Chair of Jurisprudence at Oxford, despite having no law degree and almost no publications to his name. He was quiet, precise and fond of what he called the analytical method: before you can answer a question, he thought, you must be absolutely clear about what the question means.

His lecture, published the following year in the Harvard Law Review as "Positivism and the Separation of Law and Morals", was a defence of the positivist tradition against its critics. In the same issue, the editors printed a reply by a Harvard professor called Lon Fuller. Fuller was a contract lawyer by training, the author of a famous article on reliance damages, and also something rarer: an American law professor who took philosophy seriously and believed that his colleagues' habit of treating law as a mere set of commands had something deeply wrong with it. The exchange between the two of them, which carried on in books and reviews for more than a decade, is still known simply as the Hart-Fuller debate. It remains one of the best introductions to the subject ever written, partly because both men were brilliant and partly because, unusually for academics, each understood exactly what the other was saying.

The grudge informer was at the centre of it.

What the court did, and what Hart thought it did

The account that Hart had read, in a short note published in the Harvard Law Review in 1951, reported that the German appeal court had convicted the wife on the ground that the Nazi statutes she relied on were contrary to the sound conscience and sense of justice of all decent human beings. In other words, the court had applied something like natural law: the statutes were so wicked that they had never really been law, and she could not shelter behind them.

This approach had a respectable intellectual source. Gustav Radbruch, one of the most distinguished German legal philosophers of the century, had been a positivist for most of his life and had served as Minister of Justice in the Weimar Republic. After 1945 he changed his mind. In an essay published in 1946, he argued that the positivist conviction that a law is a law had left German lawyers defenceless against statutes of arbitrary and criminal content. He proposed what became known as the Radbruch formula. In ordinary cases, he said, statute must be followed even when it is unjust, because legal certainty is itself a great value. But where the conflict between a statute and justice reaches so intolerable a degree that the statute becomes, in effect, false law, the statute must yield to justice.

Hart admired Radbruch and understood his motives. But he thought the conclusion was a mistake, and a dangerous one. The woman, he agreed, had done something monstrous, and there was a good case for punishing her. But to say that the Nazi statutes had never been law was to blur exactly the distinction that people most needed to see. In The Concept of Law, published in 1961, he put his alternative in one memorable line. We should be able to say: "This is law; but it is too iniquitous to be applied or obeyed."

Hart's point was partly about honesty. If we want to punish the informer, he argued, we face a genuine moral dilemma. On one side is the wickedness of what she did. On the other is a principle almost as precious: that people should not be punished for acts that were lawful when they committed them. The honest way to resolve the dilemma, he thought, was openly, by passing a retrospective statute and accepting that, in this case, we were choosing the lesser of two evils. To pretend that the old statute had never been law was to hide the choice from ourselves, to make it seem as if no principle had been sacrificed.

His point was also about the dangers of moral confusion. If people come to believe that only just laws are laws, they may draw the opposite and more alarming conclusion: that whatever the state commands must be just, because it is the law. Hart thought it far healthier for citizens to understand that a rule can be perfectly valid and still wicked, and that the question of whether to obey it is always, in the end, a moral question that the law itself cannot answer for them.

There was a twist. A few years later a scholar called H. O. Pappe went back to the original German judgment and showed, in an article in the Modern Law Review in 1960, that the court had not in fact declared the Nazi statute invalid at all. It had accepted that the statute was law, and convicted the wife on the narrower ground that she had used it, freely and for her own private purposes, to bring about her husband's imprisonment. The great debate had been conducted, in part, over a case that never quite happened. It did not matter as much as one might think. The question was real even if the example was flawed, and it would return, in a form nobody could dispute, a generation later.

Fuller's reply

Fuller's response to Hart was not a straightforward defence of traditional natural law. He did not argue that God or reason had written a perfect code against which all human laws must be measured. His claim was more subtle and, in some ways, more interesting.

Law, Fuller said, is not just any set of commands backed by force. It is a particular kind of enterprise: the enterprise of subjecting human conduct to the governance of rules. And that enterprise has its own internal standards, which he called the inner morality of law. In The Morality of Law, published in 1964, he illustrated them with a parable about an unhappy king called Rex, who ascends the throne full of good intentions and fails, in eight different ways, to make law at all.

First Rex tries to decide every case on its merits, without any general rules, and nobody can predict what he will do. Then he writes a code but keeps it secret. Then he publishes rules only after the events they govern, so that people discover they have broken the law after they have done so. Then he writes rules so obscure that nobody can understand them, and when he clarifies them he produces rules that contradict one another. Then he demands the impossible. Then he changes the rules so often that nobody can keep up. Finally, his own officials ignore the published rules and do as they please. At the end of the story Rex dies, old and disillusioned, and his people are relieved.

Each of Rex's failures corresponds to one of Fuller's eight principles of legality. Laws must be general; they must be published; they must not, as a rule, be retrospective; they must be clear; they must not contradict one another; they must not require the impossible; they must be reasonably stable; and the way officials actually behave must match the rules as declared. A system that fails completely on any of these, Fuller said, is not a bad legal system. It is not a legal system at all.

And here was the link with the grudge informer. The Nazi regime, Fuller pointed out, had violated these principles constantly. It passed secret laws. It punished people retrospectively. Its courts ignored statutes when it suited the party, and the party ignored the courts. Its statutes were drafted in vague terms like "the healthy sentiment of the people" precisely so that they could mean whatever the authorities wanted. A regime that treats rules with such contempt, Fuller argued, forfeits the respect that we owe to law, and post-war courts were entitled to recognise that much of what it called law was nothing of the kind.

Fuller also suggested something more hopeful: that there is a connection between the formal virtues of law and its moral content. When rulers have to state their rules openly and in general terms, and then actually apply them, it becomes harder for them to do evil. Wickedness prefers the dark. It is easier to persecute people by secret decree and administrative whim than by published, general, prospective rules applied by independent judges in open court.

Hart's response, and the poisoner

Hart was not convinced. In a review of The Morality of Law, he agreed that Fuller's eight principles were important. He simply denied that they were moral principles. They were, he said, principles of efficacy: the conditions you must satisfy if you want rules to guide behaviour successfully. Any purposive activity has principles like that. A poisoner, Hart remarked, has principles of good technique too: avoid poisons that make the victim vomit, avoid poisons whose shape and colour attract attention. Nobody would call that the inner morality of poisoning.

More seriously, Hart insisted that the principles of legality were compatible with very great iniquity. History seems to bear him out. Slavery in the American South and apartheid in South Africa were maintained, to a large extent, through clear, public, general and prospective rules, applied with dreary consistency by professional judges. If anything, their legality made them more efficient. A legal system could be scrupulously formal and utterly wicked at the same time, and that, Hart thought, was exactly why it was so important not to confuse legal validity with moral worth.

Fuller had an answer to that too, or at least the beginning of one. He did not claim that observing the principles of legality guaranteed justice, only that it made certain kinds of injustice harder, and that a legal system which respects people enough to tell them in advance what is expected of them is already treating them as responsible agents rather than as objects to be managed. There is a kind of moral respect built into the very idea of governing by rules. You can argue about how much that respect is worth, but it is not nothing.

The vehicle in the park

The debate had a second front, less dramatic but closer to what lawyers actually do every day. Hart had argued that the words of a legal rule have a core of settled meaning and a penumbra of doubt. His example has become one of the most famous in legal philosophy. Suppose a rule says: no vehicles in the park. A car is clearly a vehicle; that is the core. But what about a bicycle, a roller skate or a toy electric car? These lie in the penumbra, and here, Hart said, the judge must exercise discretion. In easy cases, the judge simply applies the rule. In hard cases, he must in a sense legislate, filling the gap by asking what the rule is for.

Fuller attacked this as well. Meaning, he said, does not live in individual words. You cannot understand a rule without understanding its purpose, even in the easy cases. Imagine, he suggested, that a group of local patriots wants to put a lorry used in the Second World War on a pedestal in the park as a war memorial. The lorry is plainly a vehicle, right in the core of the word. Yet would any sensible judge say it is forbidden? The rule was meant to stop noise and danger, not to ban memorials. Interpretation, Fuller insisted, is always a matter of purpose, and purpose is always, at some level, a matter of value.

Anyone who has read a statute closely will recognise both men in themselves. Hart is right that most of the time the words do the work and nobody argues. Fuller is right that the moment someone does argue, the words alone are rarely enough.

The question comes back

In 1990 Germany was reunified, and the question that the grudge informer had raised came back in a form that could not be dismissed as a misreported anecdote. Border guards of the former East Germany had shot and killed people trying to cross the Berlin Wall. They had acted under East German law and on orders from above, and the East German state had treated the killings as lawful. Could they now be prosecuted?

The German courts said they could, and when the matter reached the Federal Constitutional Court in 1996, it relied, in part, on the Radbruch formula: a justification so grossly contrary to fundamental principles of justice and human rights could not be recognised. In 2001 the European Court of Human Rights, in a case brought by senior East German officials who had been convicted, held that the convictions did not breach the principle that there can be no punishment without law. Radbruch's idea, half a century after he formulated it, had become part of the working law of Europe.

Whether that vindicates natural law or simply shows that positivist systems can choose to incorporate moral standards is, of course, still argued about. Later positivists, including many of Hart's own students, accepted that a legal system can make morality part of its test for validity, for example by enacting a constitution with a bill of rights. When a court strikes down a statute for violating human dignity, it is applying law and morality at once. The old dividing line has become harder to draw.

Why it matters

It would be easy to treat all this as a debate for seminar rooms. It is not.

Every legal system has to decide what its judges should do when the law, read literally, produces an outcome that seems plainly wrong. In 1889 a New York court faced the case of a young man who had poisoned his grandfather to make sure he inherited under the old man's will. The statute governing wills said nothing about murderers; on its face, he was entitled to the estate. The court refused to let him have it, relying on the principle that no one should profit from his own wrong. Was that court applying the law or overriding it? The answer depends on which side of the Hart-Fuller debate you are on, and later philosophers, Ronald Dworkin above all, built entire theories of law around cases like it.

The same question arises, in less dramatic forms, every time a lawyer argues that a contract term should not be enforced because it is unconscionable, that a regulation must be read in the light of fundamental rights, or that a long-established precedent has become unjust and should be overruled. It arises for citizens too. Should you obey a law you believe to be wrong? Hart would say that the law cannot answer that question for you, and that you must not imagine it can. Fuller would say that the respect a law deserves depends in part on whether it has been made and applied in the way that law should be. King would say, with Augustine, that an unjust law is no law at all, and then accept his prison sentence anyway, because civil disobedience, done openly and with a willingness to take the punishment, is itself a form of respect for the idea of law.

Perhaps the most useful lesson is that Hart and Fuller were less far apart than their admirers sometimes suggest. Both were liberals. Both had been appalled by what had happened in Germany. Both wanted lawyers and citizens to think harder about the moral claims of law, not less. They disagreed about where to locate the moral question: Hart wanted it outside the concept of law, as a separate question we must always ask; Fuller wanted it inside, as part of what makes law what it is. But both of them were trying to make sure that nobody could ever again say, as so many officials said after 1945, that they had simply been following the law.

That is perhaps the best reason for a young lawyer to read them. Sooner or later, in a small way or a large one, everyone who practises law is asked to apply a rule that seems wrong. It helps to have thought in advance about what you will say, and to understand that "it is the law" is the beginning of the argument, not the end of it.` },
{ id: 'd20261107-1031-5', kind: 'Reportaje', topic: 'history', title: 'The carpenter who solved the longitude problem', sub: 'Ships were sinking because sailors could not tell how far east or west they were. Parliament offered a fortune for the answer, and a self-taught clockmaker from Lincolnshire spent forty years finding it.',
body: `On the night of 22 October 1707, a British fleet returning from the Mediterranean ran onto the rocks of the Isles of Scilly, off the south-western tip of England. Four warships were lost, among them the flagship, HMS Association, and with them somewhere between 1,400 and 2,000 men, including the commander of the fleet, Admiral Sir Cloudesley Shovell. It was one of the worst disasters in the history of the Royal Navy, and it happened not in battle, nor in a hurricane, but because a group of highly experienced officers did not know where they were.

They were not careless. They were simply up against a problem that had defeated the finest minds in Europe for two hundred years. A sailor at sea could work out his latitude, how far north or south he was, with reasonable accuracy by measuring the height of the sun at noon or of the Pole Star at night. But there was no reliable way to work out his longitude, how far east or west. For that, navigators relied on dead reckoning: an educated guess based on speed, direction and time elapsed, which might be out by dozens or even hundreds of miles. Ships ran aground on coasts they believed were far away. They missed islands where they had planned to take on water, and crews died of scurvy while their captains searched.

The longitude problem was the great technical challenge of the age, and the man who finally solved it was not an astronomer, a mathematician or a gentleman of the Royal Society. He was a carpenter from Lincolnshire.

A question of time

The theory was surprisingly simple. The earth turns through 360 degrees in twenty-four hours, which means 15 degrees every hour. If a navigator knew the exact time at his home port at the very moment it was noon where he was, the difference between the two would tell him his longitude. If it was noon on board and three o'clock in the afternoon in London, he was 45 degrees west of London.

Local noon was easy to establish: you watched for the moment the sun reached its highest point. The difficulty was knowing the time back home. That required a clock that kept perfect time for weeks or months on end, aboard a ship that pitched and rolled, through heat and cold, damp and salt, changes in air pressure and the violent motion of storms. The pendulum clocks of the period, the most accurate instruments in existence, were useless at sea. Isaac Newton himself was sceptical, and suggested that the answer was more likely to come from the heavens than from a workshop.

In 1714, prompted by petitions from merchants and sea captains, Parliament passed the Longitude Act. It offered a reward of £20,000, a colossal fortune at the time, for a method of finding longitude at sea to within half a degree, with smaller prizes for less accurate methods. A body of commissioners, later known as the Board of Longitude, was appointed to judge the entries. It attracted inventors and cranks in equal measure. An earlier scheme, still discussed at the time, had involved wounded dogs and a supposedly magical powder. For half a century, nobody came close.

The carpenter

John Harrison was born in Yorkshire in 1693 and grew up in the village of Barrow upon Humber in Lincolnshire, where his father was a carpenter. He had little formal schooling. According to the family story, as a boy recovering from smallpox he was given a watch to play with, and spent hours listening to it and studying its movement. By his twenties he was building clocks, and building them almost entirely out of wood.

That sounds primitive. It was the opposite. Harrison had an extraordinary instinct for materials and an almost obsessive determination to eliminate friction, the enemy of every clockmaker, because friction required oil and oil thickened in the cold and turned to sludge over time. So he made the moving parts of his clocks from lignum vitae, a tropical hardwood so naturally oily that it needed no lubricant. He invented a new kind of escapement, the mechanism that releases the clock's energy in regular beats, which became known as the grasshopper for the way it kicked. He designed a pendulum made of alternating rods of brass and steel, which expand by different amounts as the temperature changes, so that the expansions cancelled each other out and the pendulum stayed the same length. Some of his wooden clocks are said to have kept time to within a second a month, an accuracy that the best London makers would have struggled to match.

In the late 1720s he heard about the longitude prize, and he set out to build a clock that could survive the sea.

Forty years

The first, now known as H1, was finished in 1735. It was a strange and beautiful object, a cluster of brass wheels and balances weighing over thirty kilograms, with no pendulum at all. Instead, two linked bars swung against each other, so that the motion of a ship would affect them equally and the errors would cancel out. It was tested on a voyage to Lisbon and back in 1736, and on the return journey Harrison's reckoning correctly showed that the ship was further west than the master believed.

The Board of Longitude was impressed enough to give him money to continue. Harrison, a perfectionist who seems to have been incapable of being satisfied with anything he had made, decided that H1 was not good enough, and did not even ask for a full trial. He built H2, which was bigger and heavier, and then abandoned it too. He spent the next nineteen years on H3, and in the course of it invented two devices still in use today: the bimetallic strip, which compensates for changes in temperature, and the caged roller bearing, the ancestor of the ball bearings in almost every machine on earth.

Then, in the 1750s, he changed direction completely. He had been working on a pocket watch for his own use, and he came to realise that a small, fast-beating balance might be more stable than any large mechanism. The result was H4: a silver watch about thirteen centimetres across, a fraction of the size of his earlier machines, finished in 1759 when Harrison was sixty-six years old.

In 1761 his son William took H4 on a voyage to Jamaica aboard HMS Deptford. When they arrived, after eighty-one days at sea, the watch had lost just over five seconds. That corresponds to an error in longitude of little more than a mile. It was not merely within the terms of the Act; it was many times better than the Act required.

The fight for the prize

It should have been the end of the story. It was the beginning of a long, bitter dispute.

The Board was dominated by astronomers, and astronomers had their own solution to the longitude problem. The method of lunar distances involved measuring the angle between the moon and certain stars and comparing it with tables calculated in advance for the Greenwich meridian. It was laborious, requiring hours of calculation, but it was elegant, and it relied on the clockwork of the heavens rather than the clockwork of a carpenter. Its leading champion was Nevil Maskelyne, a clergyman and astronomer who became Astronomer Royal in 1765 and was, at the same time, a member of the very Board that was judging Harrison's watch.

The commissioners were not convinced that the Jamaica result was anything more than luck, and argued over the accuracy of the time checks on arrival. They ordered a second trial, to Barbados in 1764, and H4 performed superbly again. They then demanded that Harrison hand over the watch, explain its workings in full to a panel of experts, and prove that it could be copied by other makers. Only then would they pay him half the prize. Harrison, by now in his seventies, believed, with some justice, that the rules were being changed to keep him from winning. He complied, furiously, and received £10,000 in 1765. The Board then demanded more copies and further trials before it would release the rest.

In the end it was the king who intervened. George III, a keen amateur of science, had one of Harrison's later watches, H5, tested at his private observatory at Kew in 1772, and it performed brilliantly. With royal support, Harrison petitioned Parliament directly, and in 1773 it voted him £8,750. He never formally received the longitude prize, which in fact was never officially awarded to anyone. He died in London on 24 March 1776, his eighty-third birthday.

What he left behind

Harrison's vindication came at sea. A watchmaker called Larcum Kendall made an exact copy of H4, and Captain James Cook took it on his second great voyage of exploration in the 1770s, across the Pacific and deep into the Antarctic Ocean. Cook, who had used the lunar method on his first voyage, was full of praise for the watch, which allowed him to chart coastlines with an accuracy nobody had achieved before. Within a few decades, marine chronometers based on the principles Harrison had established were standard equipment in navies and merchant fleets. The lunar method survived for many years as a backup, and Maskelyne's Nautical Almanac, first published in 1767, became a fixture of every navigator's library. In a sense both sides had won.

Harrison's clocks lay neglected for well over a century until, in the 1920s, a retired naval officer called Rupert Gould devoted years to restoring them, unpaid. They can be seen today at the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, and H1, H2 and H3 are still ticking.

There is a lesson in the story, and it is not the obvious one about a lone genius triumphing over the establishment. Harrison was a genius, and the establishment did treat him shabbily. But he was also stubborn, secretive and slow, and his own perfectionism cost him decades. Maskelyne was not simply a villain; he believed, sincerely, in a method that worked and that did not depend on expensive instruments that very few ships could afford. Great problems are rarely solved in a single flash of inspiration. They are solved by people who spend forty years filing brass in a workshop, and by the patient, unglamorous testing that finally tells you whether something works.

Every time your phone tells you exactly where you are, it does so by measuring time: the GPS satellites overhead carry atomic clocks, and your position is calculated from the tiny differences in when their signals arrive. Three centuries after the wreck on the Scilly rocks, finding your way in the world is still a question of knowing what time it is.` },
{ id: 'd20261107-1031-6', kind: 'Email', topic: 'law', title: 'Order HF-2291: a late delivery, a strike and a force majeure clause', sub: 'A Bristol distributor, a Toledo olive oil producer and their London solicitors trade emails over three delayed containers. Liquidated damages, notice clauses and a settlement, written as lawyers write.',
body: `From: Rachel Hartley, Commercial Director, Hartley & Finch Provisions Ltd (Bristol)
To: Javier Montoro, Export Manager, Olivares del Alberche S.L. (Toledo)
Subject: Order HF-2291: late delivery

Dear Javier,

I'm afraid I need to raise the delivery of order HF-2291 formally.

As you know, the contract provides for delivery of three containers of extra virgin olive oil to Avonmouth by 15 October. The first container arrived on 3 November. The second and third are, as far as we can tell, still sitting in Valencia. The timing could hardly be worse: this oil was earmarked for our Christmas gift range, and two of our major retail customers have told us that if stock isn't on their shelves by the end of the month, they will take the slots elsewhere.

Clause 9 of our agreement provides for liquidated damages of 0.5% of the price of the delayed goods for each full week of delay, up to a maximum of 10%. We will be applying that clause. I should also say that our losses go well beyond that figure, and we are taking advice on whether we can recover them.

I'd much rather sort this out between us than through lawyers. We've worked together for six years, and I'd like that to continue, but I do need a firm delivery date by Friday.

Best regards,
Rachel

From: Javier Montoro
To: Rachel Hartley
Subject: RE: Order HF-2291: late delivery

Dear Rachel,

Thank you for your email, and I'm truly sorry for the trouble this has caused you.

I must, however, point out that the delay was not our fault. The oil was bottled, packed and delivered to the port of Valencia on 26 September, well in time. From 30 September the dockworkers were on strike, and for almost three weeks no containers were loaded. When the strike ended there was enormous congestion, and the shipping line has given us a new departure date of 12 November, which should mean arrival in Avonmouth around 20 November.

Clause 14 of the contract is a force majeure clause, and it specifically mentions "strikes, lockouts or other industrial action". We therefore consider that we are excused for the period of the delay and that clause 9 does not apply.

We value our relationship with you very much and would be happy to discuss how we can help with your customers.

Kind regards,
Javier

From: Daniel Okoro, Senior Associate, Marlowe Kett LLP
To: Javier Montoro
Cc: Rachel Hartley
Subject: Hartley & Finch Provisions Ltd / Olivares del Alberche S.L.: order HF-2291

Dear Mr Montoro,

We act for Hartley & Finch Provisions Ltd in connection with the late delivery of order HF-2291. Please direct all further correspondence on this matter to us.

Our client does not accept that clause 14 excuses the delay, for three reasons.

First, clause 14 only applies where a party is "prevented" from performing by an event beyond its reasonable control. The English courts interpret force majeure clauses narrowly, and the burden is on the party relying on the clause to show that the event fell within its terms and actually caused the failure to perform. Our client understands that the strike was announced by the unions on 17 September, more than a week before the goods reached the port. It was therefore foreseeable, and alternative routes, including road freight and sailings from Bilbao, do not appear to have been considered.

Second, clause 14.3 requires the affected party to notify the other "promptly, and in any event within seven days" of becoming aware of the force majeure event, giving details of the event and its likely effect. Our client received no such notice. The first mention of the strike was in your email of 5 November, some five weeks after it began. Notice provisions of this kind are commonly treated as conditions of reliance on the clause, and our client's position is that, having failed to comply, your company cannot rely on it.

Third, clause 14.4 obliges the affected party to use "all reasonable endeavours to mitigate" the effects of the event. We would be grateful for evidence of the steps taken.

Our client therefore intends to deduct liquidated damages under clause 9 from the outstanding invoice. Our client also reserves its rights in relation to its wider losses, including loss of profit on its Christmas range.

We look forward to hearing from you within fourteen days.

Yours sincerely,
Daniel Okoro

From: Lucía Ferrer, Partner, Ferrer Whitcombe (London)
To: Daniel Okoro
Subject: RE: Hartley & Finch Provisions Ltd / Olivares del Alberche S.L.: order HF-2291

Dear Mr Okoro,

We have been instructed by Olivares del Alberche S.L. and write in response to your email of 7 November.

Our client maintains that clause 14 applies. The strike was announced on 17 September, but it was widely reported at the time that negotiations were continuing and that a settlement was expected. A threatened strike does not deprive a party of the protection of a clause that expressly covers industrial action. As for alternative routes, our client did investigate road freight; we enclose quotations showing that it would have more than doubled the cost of carriage, and that no hauliers were available at short notice in any case, because every exporter in Valencia was trying to do the same thing. Clause 14.4 requires reasonable endeavours, not the impossible.

On notice, we accept that the email of 5 November was the first written notice. However, our client's sales team spoke to your client's buying department by telephone on 2 October and explained that the port was closed. Clause 14.3 does not require notice to be given in writing. Whether that conversation satisfied the clause is, at the very least, arguable.

We also note your reference to your client's "wider losses". Clause 9.3 states that the liquidated damages payable under clause 9 "shall be the Buyer's sole and exclusive remedy for delay in delivery". Your client agreed to that allocation of risk. It cannot now claim general damages for the same delay. Even if our client were not entitled to rely on clause 14, therefore, your client's maximum recovery would be capped at the figure produced by clause 9, which our client calculates at £4,860.

Our client has no wish to litigate over a sum of that size with a valued customer. We will write separately on a without prejudice basis.

Yours sincerely,
Lucía Ferrer

From: Daniel Okoro
To: Rachel Hartley
Subject: Privileged and confidential: where we are

Rachel,

Candidly, Ferrer Whitcombe's reply is stronger than I'd hoped for. On force majeure, I still think we have the better of the argument, but it's not clear-cut. If someone in your buying team really was told about the strike on 2 October, a court may well find that the notice requirement was met in substance, and their evidence on road haulage costs takes some of the sting out of our mitigation point. I'd put our chances on force majeure at around sixty-forty in our favour.

The bigger problem is clause 9.3. I'm afraid they're right: the liquidated damages are stated to be your exclusive remedy for delay. Courts will generally enforce clauses like this between commercial parties of equal bargaining power, and there's no suggestion that 0.5% a week is a penalty; if anything, it's on the low side. So, whatever happens on force majeure, the realistic ceiling on any claim for delay is around £4,860, not the £60,000 or so of lost profit you've estimated.

Can I ask you to check with the buying team about the 2 October call? And it would be worth thinking about what you actually want out of this. If the answer is "oil on the shelves by the end of the month and a supplier we can still trust", there may be cheaper ways of getting there than a fight.

Best,
Daniel

From: Rachel Hartley
To: Daniel Okoro
Subject: RE: Privileged and confidential: where we are

Daniel,

Thanks, that's very clear, if not what I wanted to hear.

I've spoken to the team. Tom took the call on 2 October, made a note of it and then went on holiday, and nobody followed it up. So, yes, we were told. That's on us as much as them, and I'd rather not have it aired in a witness box.

What I really want is the oil. If they can get the remaining two containers to us by road from Toledo in time for our customers' deadline, I'll drop the claim for the wider losses entirely and settle for a modest discount.

R.

From: Daniel Okoro
To: Lucía Ferrer
Subject: WITHOUT PREJUDICE SAVE AS TO COSTS: order HF-2291

Dear Ms Ferrer,

Thank you for your email. Our client shares your client's wish to resolve this matter commercially and to preserve what has been a valuable relationship for both companies.

Without prejudice save as to costs, our client proposes the following:

1. Your client will arrange for the two outstanding containers to be withdrawn from the port and delivered by road to our client's warehouse in Avonmouth no later than 21 November, at your client's expense.

2. Our client will accept a reduction of 5% on the invoice price of the delayed goods, in full and final settlement of all claims arising out of the late delivery of order HF-2291.

3. Neither party will make any admission as to liability, and the terms of the settlement will remain confidential.

This offer remains open for acceptance until 5 pm on 14 November.

Yours sincerely,
Daniel Okoro

From: Lucía Ferrer
To: Daniel Okoro
Subject: RE: WITHOUT PREJUDICE SAVE AS TO COSTS: order HF-2291

Dear Mr Okoro,

Our client accepts your client's proposal, subject to one amendment: the delivery date in paragraph 1 should read 24 November, which is the earliest date on which our client's haulier can guarantee collection from Valencia and delivery to Avonmouth. Our client is also prepared to offer your client a 3% discount on its next order as a gesture of goodwill.

If that is acceptable, we will prepare a short settlement agreement for signature.

Yours sincerely,
Lucía Ferrer

From: Rachel Hartley
To: Javier Montoro
Subject: Christmas

Javier,

The lawyers tell me we have a deal, which is good news for us and rather less for them.

I'm sorry it got as formal as it did. On reflection, my first email was written by someone who'd just had a very unpleasant phone call with a supermarket buyer, and it showed. Shall we have lunch when I'm next in Madrid? I'll pay. Apparently I've got a 3% discount coming.

Rachel

From: Javier Montoro
To: Rachel Hartley
Subject: RE: Christmas

Rachel,

With pleasure. And next time there's a strike, I promise you'll hear about it in writing, in capital letters and on the same day.

Un abrazo,
Javier` }
);
